Please turn the chart above into a LIVE pinned panel backed by the integrated data source "{{source}}", instead of the current snapshot — bind it to the data source so it re-queries and refreshes every time the dashboard opens.

Use the pin_query tool, and make sure:
1. operation uses the corresponding read-only query — a SQL statement or a NoSQL operation (below; if missing, reconstruct it from the chart and context); for an http source whose list is nested in the response, set http_list_path (e.g. "data.list") yourself so rows are unpacked;
2. chart_spec is driven by the query result — never inline fixed data and never use functions/callbacks (the dashboard renders declaratively, no eval). Either (a) a simple chart {type:line/bar/area/pie/scatter, x, y[]}, or (b) for varied/needs-reshaping result shapes, {type:echarts, option} bound via an echarts dataset: declare a dataset but OMIT its source (on each re-query the dashboard injects the result rows into the first dataset.source and the query columns as dimensions), reshape rows with echarts BUILT-IN transforms (filter/sort/...), and map dimensions with series.encode. IMPORTANT — derive everything from the ACTUAL data: first call run_data_query once to read the exact returned column names, then use ONLY those names in dataset dimensions / series.encode / transforms, and auto-generate any needed transform (filter / sort / aggregate) from the result shape YOURSELF — do not ask the user. Never encode or sort a dimension the query does not return: that throws and breaks the panel;
3. name the panel "{{title}}".

